      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we will use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve. The formula is given by:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the derivatives \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\nx = 2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\nWe differentiate \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\left( 2 \\cos t \\right)^2 + \\left( -2 \\sin t \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFactor out the 4:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nUse the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt\n\\]\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt\n\\]\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nNow, integrate \\(2\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt\n\\]\n\\[\nL = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi}\n\\]\n\\[\nL = 2 (\\pi - 0)\n\\]\n\\[\nL = 2 \\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
